swehouse.c: fill the Gauquelin sectors the Porphyry fallback leaves behind

Gauquelin sectors return 36 cusps, and swephprg says so in red: "the array
cusps must provide space for 13 doubles (declare as cusp[13]) ... With house
system 'G' ... declare it as cusp[37]". Inside the polar circle 'G' cannot be
computed, so CalcH() falls back to Porphyry and returns ERR, and
swe_houses_armc_ex2() then copies twelve cusps instead of thirty-six:

    // on failure, we only have 12 Porphyry cusps
    if (retc < 0) {
      ito = 12;
      if (serr != NULL) strcpy(serr, h.serr);
    }
    for (i = 1; i <= ito; i++) {
      cusp[i] = h.cusp[i];
      if (h.do_hspeed) cusp_speed[i] = h.cusp_speed[i];
    }

Elements 13 to 36 of both arrays are never written. The caller sized them as
the documentation requires, so on that path it reads back whatever it had
there, which for an ordinary automatic array is uninitialised stack. Nothing
in the return signals which elements are valid: the caller gets ERR and the
message "within polar circle, switched to Porphyry", which says the house
system changed, not that two thirds of the array it passed were left alone.

Found by writing a sentinel into every element before the call and looking for
survivors afterwards. 24 positions and 24 speeds survive on every 'G' call
inside the polar circle; outside it none do, and no other house system leaves
anything at all across 663,520 random calls spanning 1000 BC to 4100 AD.

swetest does not show this one either: for 'G' inside the polar circle it prints
12 house lines instead of its usual 36, so the affected sectors never reach the
output. Filling the array with a sentinel first is what makes it visible.

One consequence to know about rather than discover: after this patch the ERR
path writes elements 13 to 36, where before it wrote only 1 to 12. A caller
who ignored swephprg and declared cusp[13] was overrunning on every successful
'G' call already, but happened to survive this particular failure path; now it
overruns here too, and an address sanitizer will say so. The documented size
is the fix for that caller, not this patch.

    #include "swephexp.h"
    #include <stdio.h>
    int main(void) {
      double c[37], a[10], cs[37]; char s[256]; int i;
      for (i = 0; i < 37; i++) c[i] = -8.8e88;
      swe_houses_armc_ex2(286.3561, 80.0, 23.4392911, 'G', c, a, cs, NULL, s);
      for (i = 13; i <= 36; i++)
        if (c[i] == -8.8e88) { printf("cusp %d never written\n", i); return 0; }
      printf("all 36 written\n");
      return 0;
    }

    hsys 'G', armc 286.3561, eps 23.4392911:

      geolat 45        retc  0                                       0 unwritten
      geolat 60        retc  0                                       0 unwritten
      geolat 70        retc -1  within polar circle, switched to...  24 unwritten
      geolat 81.0025   retc -1  within polar circle, switched to...  24 unwritten

They are cleared to zero, before ito is clamped so that the loop bound is still
the one the caller asked for. Zero is chosen over leaving them because the
elements are documented as belonging to the caller's array and a defined value
cannot be mistaken for a computed one; a caller that already ignores everything
past the twelfth cusp on ERR is unaffected either way.

Clearing them is not enough on its own. The three sidereal helpers compute
ito = 36 for 'G' from hsys alone and then subtract the ayanamsa from cusp[1] to
cusp[ito] whatever the call returned, so the cleared elements come back shifted
and look like ordinary cusps rather than the "not computed" they are. Each
helper now clamps ito to 12 when the call failed, the same way
swe_houses_armc_ex2() already does. Sidereal 'G' at geolat 70, Fagan/Bradley:

      cusp[13]   before   56.26356987   (the caller's own -999, shifted)
      cusp[13]   cleared only  335.26356987
      cusp[13]   with the clamp   0.00000000

This touches only the ERR path of a house system that returns more than twelve
cusps, which today is 'G' alone. Positions and speeds on every successful call
are untouched: bit-identical over 663,520 random (date, latitude, longitude)
configurations across all 26 house letters, with identical return codes and
error strings.

This is independent of the cusp speed patches and of the Sunshine and APC
position fixes, and may be applied in any order with them.

